  <section class="feature">
    <div class="feature-prose">
      <h2>The check reaches past your code</h2>
      <p>
        This script has three mistakes, and none of them are weir: a
        tool's flag, checked against a generated signature
        (<code>weir add sig bicep</code>); a manifest's key, checked
        against a vendored schema (<code>weir add schema</code>); and
        a program name, checked against your PATH. One report, every
        finding located — and nothing ran.
      </p>
      <p class="fine">
        Nothing on this page is a mock-up — every quoted output comes
        from running the shipped binary, verified in CI.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-demo">
      <Code code={beat1Weir} lang={weirGrammar} themes={queilThemes} defaultColor={false} />
      <figure class="term">
        <figcaption>$ weir check deploy.weir</figcaption>
        <pre class="refusal wrap">{beat1Check}</pre>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="feature">
    <div class="feature-prose">
      <h2>A real CLI, free</h2>
      <p>
        Declare the shape; the interface is derived. The
        <code>///</code> docs become the help text, so you cannot
        forget to update <code>--help</code> — and unknown flags,
        missing requireds and bad values arrive together, in one
        error, before any effect.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-demo">
      <Code code={beat2Weir} lang={weirGrammar} themes={queilThemes} defaultColor={false} />
      <figure class="term">
        <figcaption>$ weir ship.weir --help</figcaption>
        <pre class="report">{beat2Help}</pre>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <h2>Two ingredients, removed</h2>
  <p>
    Two bash mechanisms wreck machines. One is an unquoted variable
    <em>splitting</em> into words — still quoted away by hand in
    production, as pgpool did to an <code>rm -rf</code> in a
    node-recovery script in <a href="https://github.com/pgpool/pgpool2/commit/58446a6">September
    2025</a>, a maintainer catching it before it fired. The other is a
    path <em>glued</em> from pieces that came apart; Valve shipped that
    in 2015, and it wiped real drives:
  </p>
  <div class="hero-band">
    <div class="hero-pair">
      <div class="hero-cell">
        <p class="hero-label">steam.sh, shipped:</p>
        <Code code={steamBash} lang="bash" themes={queilThemes} defaultColor={false} />
        <p class="hero-caption">
          When the folder moved, <code>&#36;(…)</code> came back
          <em>empty</em> — <code>"$STEAMROOT/"*</code> became
          <code>/*</code>, and the last line deleted everything users
          owned, <a href="https://github.com/ValveSoftware/steam-for-linux/issues/3671">mounted
          backup drives included</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-cell">
        <p class="hero-label">The same shape, in weir:</p>
        <Code code={spliceWeir} lang={weirGrammar} themes={queilThemes} defaultColor={false} />
        <figure class="term">
          <figcaption>$ weir check steam.weir</figcaption>
          <pre class="refusal">{spliceRefusal}</pre>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>
    Weir removes both ingredients: a spliced value is always exactly
    one argv word, and gluing is unrepresentable — neither failure is
    a mistake weir detects, both are sentences weir cannot say.
  </p>

  <section class="feature">
    <div class="feature-prose">
      <h2>See what a script can do before you run it</h2>
      <p>
        Command names are literal and nothing expands inside argv, so
        a script's reach is statically knowable —
        <code>weir check --can</code> reports it, with a source line
        for every claim.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-demo">
      <figure class="term">
        <figcaption>$ weir check --can tools/fuzz.weir</figcaption>
        <pre class="report">{canReport}</pre>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <h2>What falls out</h2>
  <ul class="falls-out">
    <li>
      <a href="/docs/guide/#parallelism"><code>within proc</code></a> —
      a background process gets a scope, never a <code>&amp;</code>;
      the tree is killed and reaped on every exit.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="/docs/guide/#secrets-tokens-that-cannot-leak-into-logs"><code>Secret</code></a> —
      a token that cannot reach a log line, a shown record, or the
      wire; <code>Secret.reveal</code> is the only exit, so the audit
      is the call sites.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="/docs/guide/#retrying-and-polling"><code>retry</code> and <code>poll</code></a> —
      bounded loops; an unbounded loop is unrepresentable, and
      <code>watch=</code> fails the wait the moment the child dies,
      with its last output.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="/docs/guide/#exit-codes-from-command-to-value">exit codes as data</a> —
      <code>| complete</code> and <code>| succeeds</code> make a
      command's outcome a value, and <code>Seq.exactlyOne</code> says
      <em>one line</em> where <code>head</code> would silently accept
      two.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="/docs/guide/#commands-and-processes"><code>within lock</code></a> —
      an advisory file lock the kernel releases on any death,
      <code>kill -9</code> included.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="/docs/tooling/#yaml-schemas">the <code>yaml</code> block</a> —
      splices are nodes, never text, so YAML injection is
      unrepresentable — the argv law, one format over.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why not the alternatives</h2>
  <p>
    In the author's words: <strong>bash</strong> has no types — a
    misspelled command, an unset variable, a value that splits into two
    words all wait until the shell reaches that line, mid-run.
    <strong>PowerShell</strong> compounds its
    quirks at tool scale and is not typed where it counts.
    <strong>F# scripts</strong> had the types — weir is F#-shaped
    because they were closest — but not the start-up time, the
    caching, or identical behaviour across Linux CI and macOS/Windows
    dev machines. <strong>Nushell</strong> is nice, but its type
    system is still being worked out. Weir selects from F#, which
    works, and adds the shell-specific parts.
  </p>

  <h2>Provenance</h2>
  <p>
    weir was written almost entirely by an AI agent, directed by one
    person through plans, reviews and rulings — about two months,
    part time. That is a statement about economics, not capability.
    What makes it checkable rather than a claim: every fenced example
    in the docs executes in CI (error demos must <em>fail</em>); an
    F# fidelity oracle referees every divergence claim; an external
    referee pushes 103 hostile payloads through PyYAML and
    Python's <code>json</code>; and five adversarial reviews sit,
    findings and all, in the
    <a href="https://github.com/weir-shell/weir/blob/main/docs/DECISIONS.md">public ledger</a>.
  </p>
